{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>There shall be none found in your houses.</b> From where do we derive the boundaries? Scripture says: ״בְּכָל גְּבוּלֶךָ״ (Exodus 13). What does Scripture teach by ״בְּבָתֵּיכֶם״? Just as your house is in your domain, so too your boundary — that which is in your domain. This excludes the leaven of a non-Jew that is with an Israelite who did not accept responsibility for it (Mekhilta)." "<b>For whoever eats anything leavened.</b> To punish karet for se'or — but was He not already punishing for chametz? Rather, so you should not say: chametz, which is fit for eating — He punished for it; se'or, which is not fit for eating — He would not punish for it. And if He punished for se'or but not for chametz, I would say: se'or, which ferments others — He punished for it; chametz, which does not ferment others — He would not punish for it. Therefore both were stated (Mekhilta)." "<b>Both the stranger and the native of the land.</b> Since the miracle was done for Israel, it was necessary to include the converts (ibid.)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:51:42.464332641Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}